## Connecting an MCP Server to Copilot

VS Code reads MCP server configuration from two places:

**1. Workspace-scoped (just this project):** `.vscode/mcp.json` in your project folder
**2. User-scoped (all projects):** Your VS Code `settings.json` under the `mcp.servers` key

For most team use, workspace-scoped (`.vscode/mcp.json`) is recommended — it version-controls the config with your project and every teammate picks it up automatically.

### The Config File Format

Create (or open) `.vscode/mcp.json` in your project and add this structure:

```json
{
  "servers": {
    "server-name": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "package-name"],
      "env": {
        "KEY": "value"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

- **`command`** — the executable VS Code will run to start the MCP server (`npx`, `uvx`, `node`, `python`, etc.)
- **`args`** — arguments passed to that command
- **`env`** — environment variables the server needs (API keys, URLs, etc.)

VS Code starts and stops the MCP server automatically when you open Agent Mode. You don't run it manually.
